{short:"Brain",label:"How We Train the Brain",parts:[{label:"The starting point",title:"How We Train the Brain",body:["I’m interested in what happens when deliberate control becomes less reliable under pressure. The work looks beyond what an athlete can explain and toward the responses that actually appear when the moment becomes demanding."],quote:"“People work on their body more than they work on their brain, and that’s why their skill level stagnates or declines.” — Conor McGregor"}]},
{short:"Clear mind",label:"How to Clear an Athlete’s Mind",parts:[{label:"The principle",title:"A Clear Mind Is Not an Empty Mind",textHeading:"WHAT MATTERS",body:["I don't treat every thought as a problem that needs to be solved. Under pressure, the important question is whether the athlete can recognize what deserves attention and what does not.","A clear mind is useful because it creates room for the performance itself. It is not about having fewer thoughts; it is about not allowing every thought to become a performance problem."]}]},
{short:"Emotions",label:"EMOTIONS",parts:[{label:"The competitive state",title:"Emotion Does Not Have to Drive Behavior",body:["I don't ask athletes to eliminate emotion. The more useful question is whether performance can remain organized when emotion is present.","The deeper performance question is not “How do we make the athlete feel nothing?” It is whether the athlete can stay connected to the demands of the moment instead of letting the emotional state decide what happens next."]}]},
{short:"Visualize",label:"Visualization and Self-Talk",parts:[{label:"Tools, not rituals",title:"Context Before Technique",body:["Visualization and self-talk can be useful, but I don't treat them as universal solutions or rituals that should be applied regardless of the athlete or the problem.","Their value depends on what the athlete actually needs to change. The technique is secondary; understanding the performance problem comes first."]}]},
{short:"Misconceptions",label:"Common Misconceptions in Mental Performance",parts:[{label:"What mental performance is not",title:"Beyond Mental Tricks",body:["Mental toughness is not the absence of fear. Caring about results is not the same as being controlled by them. And feeling better in a session is not the same as changing how an athlete performs.","I’m interested in changes that survive contact with real training, competition and pressure—not another idea an athlete has to remember when things go wrong."]}]},
{short:"Approach",label:"My Approach to Mental Performance",parts:[{label:"The environment",title:"Performance Work Belongs in the Environment",body:["Mental performance should not live in a room separate from the sport. I work close to the coaching staff and the training environment, where the demands of performance are visible rather than theoretical.","The relationship is demanding but collaborative: honest conversations, clear standards, athlete ownership, and a willingness to look at what actually happens rather than what should happen.","I’m not interested in making athletes dependent on a mental coach. The goal is to leave the athlete more capable, more responsible, and better able to perform without psychological support becoming a crutch."]}]}
